    "description": "Specialist blogs place sustained attention on a subject within the wider tradition of Persian online publishing. Historical research documents a diverse, interconnected blogging culture, but does not establish which specialist publications remain active today.",
    "tags": ["Persian blogs", "Specialist publishing", "Digital history", "Literature", "Online communities", "Media literacy"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T08:31:10Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T08:31:10Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Specialist Blogs and Persian Online Publishing | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore the historical foundations of Persian specialist blogging, its linked communities, and the limits of older evidence for understanding today's publications."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "format-and-subject",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A publishing format with many subjects",
        "content": "A specialist blog is best approached through its subject and editorial practice, rather than its appearance alone. Encyclopaedia Iranica defines blogs as regularly updated collections of posts, usually displayed with the newest first. It describes both individual and group authorship, alongside blogs maintained by larger organizations. Its account identifies subjects including technology, sport, philosophy, culture, and literature, demonstrating that Persian blogging cannot be reduced to political commentary. [s1]\n\nFor this page, specialization means sustained attention to a field. That is an editorial distinction, not a certification of expertise: a writer may examine a narrow subject carefully, while another publication may adopt specialist language without showing its evidence. Readers should therefore look at the work itself, including its sources, corrections, and treatment of uncertainty.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-foundations",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From personal posts to connected discussion",
        "content": "Iranica identifies Hossein Derakhshan’s Persian blogging tutorial in 2001 as an important early development. Its historical account describes a Persian-language network extending across Iran and the diaspora, with writers linking to other posts, maintaining lists of recommended blogs, and exchanging comments. These practices made individual websites part of wider conversations. Literature, including poetry, was a significant strand of that environment. [s1]\n\nThe entry also warns against interpreting blogging solely as a liberating technology used by political opponents of the state. Its discussion includes conservative participants, disputes over acceptable language, and unequal participation. This is useful historical context for specialist publishing: an online community can make room for exchange while also developing conventions about whose knowledge and voice count. The entry’s last stated update is in 2012, so its description should not be read as a current directory. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "mapping-communities",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What network research can establish",
        "content": "A Harvard Berkman Center study published in 2008 combined computational mapping with human and automated content analysis. Its abstract describes a varied Persian blogosphere encompassing politics, human rights, religion, poetry, and popular culture. It identified broad network formations, including a literature-and-poetry grouping and mixed networks, rather than finding a single uniform public. [s2]\n\nThat approach helps explain why a specialist blog should be read in relation to other publications. Links can reveal shared interests, disagreement, or affiliation, while a writer’s references can show which conversations inform the work. However, a link is not automatically an endorsement, and a network grouping is not a professional credential. The study provides a historical model of connected discussion, not a verified classification of today’s specialist websites. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "evaluating-specialization",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Read for expertise and continuity",
        "content": "For a reader discovering a specialist publication, practical questions include whether the author’s relevant experience is explained, whether original documents are linked, and whether older posts remain available with dates intact. A technical article should identify the circumstances under which its explanation applies; a historical essay should make its sources distinguishable from interpretation. These are assessment criteria, not claims that every blog follows them.\n\nThe older research establishes that Persian blogging supported diverse subject communities and substantial interaction. It does not establish which specialist blogs are active in October 2026, which have moved to other formats, or how audiences divide between Iran and the diaspora. Until contemporary publications and archives are inspected, the defensible account is a historical foundation accompanied by a reading method. A current guide requires named examples whose specialist work can be evaluated directly. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
